# openfortiGUI FortiGate test lab -- configuration
#
# Copy to "lab.env" (which git ignores) and adjust as needed. Every value can
# also be set as an environment variable; the environment always wins over this
# file.
#
#   cp tests/fortigate-vm/lab.env.example tests/fortigate-vm/lab.env

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Work directory of the lab. Deliberately OUTSIDE the repository so that the
# non-redistributable Fortinet image can never end up in git.
#OFGUI_LAB_DIR="$HOME/.cache/openfortigui-testlab"

# Fortinet KVM image (ZIP containing fortios.qcow2). Without this, the newest
# $HOME/FGT_VM64_KVM-*.kvm.zip is used.
#FGT_IMAGE_ZIP="$HOME/FGT_VM64_KVM-v7.4.12.M-build2902-FORTINET.out.kvm.zip"

# FortiGate VM license (.lic) -- EFFECTIVELY REQUIRED.
# An unlicensed VM accepts the SSL-VPN configuration but does not start the
# daemon: port 10443 stays closed and 'diagnose vpn ssl list' reports
# "Connection refused". Details in TESTS.md, section "VM license".
# If the path is set here, 'testlab up' installs the license into the golden
# image right away; afterwards use 'testlab license <file>'.
#FGT_LICENSE=""

# The openfortiGUI binary under test. Default: the CMake build in the repo,
# otherwise the installed version from $PATH.
#OFGUI_BIN="$REPO_ROOT/cmake-build-debug/bin/openfortigui"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VM
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

#FGT_VM_NAME="ofgt-lab"
#FGT_CPUS=1                     # evaluation mode allows only 1 vCPU
#FGT_MEM_MB=2048                # evaluation mode allows only 2 GB
#FGT_MACHINE="q35"              # fallback: pc
#FGT_NIC_MODEL="virtio-net-pci" # fallback: e1000
#FGT_LOG_DISK_GB=0              # >0 creates a second disk for FortiOS logs

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# "Outside": host <-> FortiGate port1 (the VPN gateway)
#LAB_BR_OUT="ofgt-out"
#LAB_TAP_OUT="ofgt-tap0"
#LAB_OUT_NET="10.99.99.0/24"
#LAB_HOST_IP="10.99.99.1"
#FGT_WAN_IP="10.99.99.10"

# "Inside": FortiGate port2 <-> netns holding the test target. The host
# deliberately has NO IP here -- the target is reachable through the tunnel only.
#LAB_BR_IN="ofgt-in"
#LAB_TAP_IN="ofgt-tap1"
#LAB_NETNS="ofgt-inside"
#FGT_LAN_IP="10.99.10.1"
#LAB_INSIDE_IP="10.99.10.50"
#LAB_INSIDE_PORT=8080

# The FortiGate's SSL-VPN tunnel pool (default object SSLVPN_TUNNEL_ADDR1)
#LAB_POOL_FIRST="10.212.134.200"
#LAB_POOL_LAST="10.212.134.210"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FortiGate / VPN credentials
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

#FGT_HOSTNAME="FGT-LAB"
#FGT_ADMIN_USER="admin"
#FGT_ADMIN_PASS="LabAdmin#2024"
#FGT_ADMIN_SPORT=443            # admin HTTPS
#FGT_SSLVPN_PORT=10443          # SSL-VPN (openfortiGUI's UI default)

#VPN_USER="vpnuser"
#VPN_PASS="LabVpn#2024"
#VPN_GROUP="sslvpn-lab"
#VPN_PORTAL="lab-portal"

# NAT in the ssl.root -> port2 policy. "0" tests real routing (the pool IP is
# visible in the inside network), "1" is the more forgiving mode.
#FGT_POLICY_NAT=0

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The DNS test is opt-in: openfortivpn writes to /etc/resolv.conf directly here
# (RESOLVCONF_PATH is compiled empty), which on this host is a symlink into the
# systemd-resolved stub. The test case backs the file up and restores it, but by
# default it stays off.
#OFGUI_TEST_DNS=0

#TIMEOUT_BOOT=300               # seconds until the FortiOS login prompt
#TIMEOUT_TLS=180                # seconds until :10443 speaks TLS
#TIMEOUT_CONNECT=60             # seconds until "Tunnel is up and running."
#TIMEOUT_RECONNECT=120          # seconds until the second tunnel-up
